The run declares exactly ONE substrate, `local`, and the request is served by
`remote`.

This is the case the field's own doc comment exists for: the reference is
required even when a run declares one substrate, precisely so that it can
never be inferred from a count. With one declared it is tempting to read an
unresolvable reference as a typo for the only candidate, or to skip the check
because there is nothing to choose between -- and a record that resolves a
reference by counting is a record whose rows are not self-describing.

Its absence was found by a fresh-instance review, which relaxed the check to
fire only above one substrate and watched all 617 tests stay green.
